</style></head><body><header><div class="eyebrow">Entrepreneurship · Browning High School</div><h1>Why Would Someone Choose You?</h1><div class="sub">Week 5 · Day 1 — Unit V: Marketing — Value Proposition</div></header><div class="timestrip"><div class="ts-login">0:00–8:00<br>Login + Typing</div><div class="ts-review">8:00–11:00<br>Choral Recall</div><div class="ts-di">11:00–29:00<br>DI Instruction</div><div class="ts-practice">29:00–40:00<br>Practice / SPED Pull-Aside</div><div class="ts-exit">40:00–45:00<br>Exit + Logoff</div></div><div class="wrap"><div class="note goal"><span class="label">Unit Goal Connection</span>Opens the Value Proposition block. Sentence three of last week's validated idea ("the reason my customers choose me") gets a name and a quality check: specific, relevant, unique. The original corporate analysis worksheets are replaced by students analyzing their own idea plus local examples. Financial literacy touch: I. Earning Income; II. Spending — people spend money where they see value.</div><h2 class="section">0:00–8:00 · Login &amp; Typing Practice</h2><p>Standard routine.</p><h2 class="section">8:00–11:00 · Choral Recall</h2><div class="script"><p class="t">Friday you wrote three sentences. Pull out sentence three, the reason my customers choose me, and read it silently.</p><p class="t">Quick fire. An MVP is...</p><p class="cr">"The smallest version that still tests the idea."</p><p class="t">Today sentence three gets a name and a quality check.</p></div><h2 class="section">11:00–29:00 · DI Instruction — I Do / We Do / You Do</h2><h3 class="subsection">I Do — What a Value Proposition Is</h3><div class="script"><p class="t">A <span class="k">value propositio
n</span> is a statement that tells the customer the benefit you offer and why they should choose you over their other options. Say it with me:</p><p class="cr">"A value proposition says why someone should choose you."</p><p class="t">A strong one has three qualities. Say them with me:</p><p class="cr">"Specific. Relevant. Unique."</p><p class="t"><span class="k">Specific</span> means it says exactly what you do. <span class="k">Relevant</span> means it fits a problem your customer really has. <span class="k">Unique</span> means it is different from what they can already get.</p><p class="a">Write a weak and a strong example on the board. Weak: "We have the best quality." Strong: "Fresh flowers for any occasion, without the drive to another town." Ask which one is specific, relevant, and unique. Tell students the strong example is invented to show the pattern, not a claim about a real business.</p><p class="t">There are three kinds of value proposition. <span class="k">Functional</span> is a practical benefit: saves time, saves money, gets it done. <span class="k">Emotional</span> is how it makes you feel: proud, safe, connected. <span class="k">Societal</span> is good for the community or the land. Say them:</p><p class="cr">"Functional. Emotional. Societal."</p></div><h3 class="subsection">We Do — Naming the Type</h3><div class="script"><p class="t">I will read a statement. Show me one finger for functional, two for emotional, three for societal.</p><p class="a">Read: "Fixes your bike in one day." (1) / "Makes you feel proud of where you come from." (2) / "Every sale helps pay for youth sports." (3) / "Saves you a two-hour drive." (1). Discuss any split votes.</p><p class="t">Can one value proposition be more than one type?</p><p class="s">[Yes]</p><p class="t">Right. Handmade beadwork could save someone a trip, feel like family and tradition, and support a local artist all at once. The strongest ones often do more than one.</p><p class="t">Think about Rock'N Outlaw. Feed for your animals in the same place you already shop. Which type could that be?</p><p class="s">[Functional, it saves a trip]</p><p class="a">Frame this as a guess. Students are inferring the value, not reporting what the business says about itself.</p></div><div class="note channel"><span class="label">Learning Channel</span>See/Say into Think/Write. The finger vote lets every student commit to an answer out loud before writing their own.</div><h3 class="subsection">You Do — Guided Practice</h3><div class="script"><p class="t">Take your sentence three from Friday. Check it: is it specific, relevant, unique? Circle the quality that is weakest, then rewrite the sentence to fix it. Then label its type: functional, emotional, societal, or more than one.</p></div><div class="note comprehension"><span class="label">Comprehension Tip</span>Watch for generic claims like "best quality" or "low prices." These sound like value propositions but are not specific or unique. Ask "compared to what the customer already has, what is different?"</div><div class="note autism"><span class="label">Autism Observation Tip</span>"Unique" can be read literally as one-of-a-kind, which almost nothing is. Give the working meaning directly: different from the options your customer already has.</div><h2 class="section">29:00–40:00 · Practice — Two Tracks Running Concurrently</h2><h3 class="subsection">Main Class — Independent Practice</h3><p>Students finish the rewrite and type label. Early finishers write a second version aimed at a different type (if theirs was functional, try emotional) and decide which is stronger for their audience.</p><div class="sped-block"><h3 class="subsection">SPED Pull-Aside — "Who Can I Help?"</h3><p style="margin-top:0;"><strong>Format:</strong> One small group, teacher-led, 2nd-grade reading level.</p><div class="sped-script"><p class="t">A business helps people. Look at these people.</p><p class="a">Picture cards: an elder, a little kid, a neighbor, a coach.</p><p class="t">What could you help each person with?</p><p class="s">[names something for each]</p><p class="t">Now say it like a business owner: "I help ____ with ____." Try one.</p><p class="a">Model once, then each student says the sentence for one card. Accept simple answers. The goal is naming who and how.</p><p class="t">That is the start of a value proposition. Who you help, and how.</p></div></div><h2 class="section">40:00–45:00 · Exit Check</h2><div class="script"><p class="t">One sentence: what type is your value proposition, functional, emotional, or societal, and why?</p><p class="a">Not Yet / Developing / Fluent per student.</p></div><table class="assess"><tr><th>Student</th><th>Not Yet</th><th>Developing</th><th>Fluent</th></tr><tr><td colspan="4" style="color:#888;">(roster rows — printed version)</td></tr></table><div class="identity"><h3>Your Reason Already Counts</h3><p><span class="cap">Important names are capitalized</span> — and you are an important person. The reason someone would choose you is not something you invent from nothing. It comes from what you already know about your community. Tomorrow we get specific about exactly who it is for.</p></div></div></body></html>
