</style></head><body><header><div class="eyebrow">Entrepreneurship · Browning High School</div><h1>Who Is This Really For?</h1><div class="sub">Week 5 · Day 2 — Target Audience — Specific, Not Everyone</div></header><div class="timestrip"><div class="ts-login">0:00–8:00<br>Login + Typing</div><div class="ts-review">8:00–11:00<br>Choral Recall</div><div class="ts-di">11:00–29:00<br>DI Instruction</div><div class="ts-practice">29:00–40:00<br>Practice / SPED Pull-Aside</div><div class="ts-exit">40:00–45:00<br>Exit + Logoff</div></div><div class="wrap"><div class="note goal"><span class="label">Unit Goal Connection</span>Students narrow a broad audience into a specific first audience, building on the Customer Segments work from Week 3 and the test results from Week 4. Financial literacy touch: II. Spending — a specific audience has specific needs and a specific budget.</div><div class="note iefa"><span class="label">IEFA Connection</span>Community-specific audiences such as elders, youth, ranchers, tourists, families, students, and Blackfoot-language learners are more useful than generic labels like age ranges. Let students name real groups they know rather than supplying them.</div><h2 class="section">0:00–8:00 · Login &amp; Typing Practice</h2><p>Standard routine.</p><h2 class="section">8:00–11:00 · Choral Recall</h2><div class="script"><p class="t">Yesterday. A value proposition is...</p><p class="cr">"A statement of why someone should choose you."</p><p class="t">Three qualities?</p><p class="cr">"Specific. Relevant. Unique."</p><p class="t">Relevant to who? That is today's question.</p></div><h2 class="section">11:00–29:00 · DI Instruction — I Do / We Do / You Do</h2><h3 class="subsection">I Do — Narrowing an Audience</h3><div class="script"><p class="t">Your <span class="k">target audience</span> is the specific group of people most likely to need what you offer. Say it with me:</p><p class="cr">"A target audience is the specific group that needs it most."</p><p class="t">Everyone is not a target audience. If your answer is everyone, you cannot write a specific message, pick a place to sell, or set a price. Watch me narrow mine in three steps.</p><p class="a">Model on the board. Step 1, who has the problem: people who need rides. Step 2, in what situation: elders who no longer drive. Step 3, what do they do now: they wait for family who work during the day. Combine: elders in this community who no longer drive and depend on family who work during the day.</p><p class="t">Notice the narrow audience makes everything easier. I know where to find them and what they care about.</p><p class="t">Remember Rock'N Outlaw? Adding feed did not just add a product. It added a new group of customers, people with animals to feed. You start with one audience and grow from there. Starting narrow is not shutting people out.</p></div><h3 class="subsection">We Do — Three Steps</h3><div class="script"><p class="t">I will give you a broad audience. We narrow it together: who, what situation, what they do now.</p><p class="a">Start with "teenagers." Narrow with the class to "high school athletes who travel to games," then to "athletes who travel and want food that fits a tight budget." Repeat with a class suggestion such as elders, tourists, or ranchers. Let students call out each narrowing step.</p><p class="cr">"Not everyone. Someone specific."</p></div><div class="note autism"><span class="label">Autism Observation Tip</span>Students who want to know where "specific" ends and "too narrow" begins deserve a working rule: could you name three real people who fit it? If yes, it is specific enough.</div><div class="note comprehension"><span class="label">Comprehension Tip</span>Watch for students who "narrow" only by adding demographic labels ("girls ages 14 to 17"). Push for situation and behavior: what are they doing when they run into the problem?</div><h3 class="subsection">You Do — Guided Practice</h3><div class="script"><p class="t">Write your target audience in three steps: who has the problem, what situation, what they do now. Then check your Week 4 test. Did the people you asked match this audience? If not, adjust the audience, or plan a new question for the right people.</p></div><h2 class="section">29:00–40:00 · Practice — Two Tracks Running Concurrently</h2><h3 class="subsection">Main Class — Independent Practice</h3><p>Students write their three-step audience and compare it to their Week 4 test results. Early finishers name three real people who fit their audience.</p><div class="sped-block"><h3 class="subsection">SPED Pull-Aside — "Who Is This Really For?"</h3><p style="margin-top:0;"><strong>Format:</strong> One small group, teacher-led, 2nd-grade reading level.</p><div class="sped-script"><p class="t">Some things are for some people. A fishing pole is not for everyone. Who is it for?</p><p class="s">[Someone who fishes]</p><p class="a">Picture cards. Match items (fishing pole, warm coat, school backpack, cat toy) to people (someone who fishes, someone in the snow, a student, a cat owner). Then have each student say: "My idea is for ____." Help them name a real group.</p><p class="t">Good. Knowing who it is for is the first step.</p></div></div><h2 class="section">40:00–45:00 · Exit Check</h2><div class="script"><p class="t">One sentence: who is your idea really for?</p><p class="a">Not Yet / Developing / Fluent per student.</p></div><table class="assess"><tr><th>Student</th><th>Not Yet</th><th>Developing</th><th>Fluent</th></tr><tr><td colspan="4" style="color:#888;">(roster rows — printed version)</td></tr></table><div class="identity"><h3>You Know Who Needs This</h3><p><span class="cap">Important names are capitalized</span> — and you are an important person. Naming who your idea is for is not guesswork. You live in this community, and you already know who has this problem. Tomorrow we figure out how to reach them.</p></div></div></body></html>
