</style></head><body><header><div class="eyebrow">Entrepreneurship · Browning High School</div><h1>Finding Your People</h1><div class="sub">Week 5 · Day 3 — Precision Teaching Fluency Day + Channels</div></header><div class="timestrip"><div class="ts-login">0:00–8:00<br>Login + Typing</div><div class="ts-review">8:00–11:00<br>Choral Recall</div><div class="ts-di">11:00–29:00<br>DI Instruction</div><div class="ts-practice">29:00–40:00<br>Practice / SPED Pull-Aside</div><div class="ts-exit">40:00–45:00<br>Exit + Logoff</div></div><div class="wrap"><div class="note goal"><span class="label">Unit Goal Connection</span>Precision Teaching day. Fluency on value proposition, target audience, functional, emotional, societal. Channels gets a short I Do first since it is new. Financial literacy touch: II. Spending. Where you sell shapes how much it costs to reach people.</div><h2 class="section">0:00–8:00 · Login &amp; Typing Practice</h2><p>Standard routine.</p><h2 class="section">8:00–11:00 · Choral Recall</h2><div class="script"><p class="t">Quick fire. A value proposition is...</p><p class="cr">"A statement of why someone should choose you."</p><p class="t">A target audience is...</p><p class="cr">"The specific group that needs it most."</p><p class="t">Today, one new word, then fast and accurate on everything so far.</p></div><h2 class="section">11:00–29:00 · Channels Mini-Lesson + Fluency Rounds</h2><h3 class="subsection">I Do — How People Actually Find You</h3><div class="script"><p class="t">A <span class="k">channel</span> is how your customer actually finds out about you and gets what you sell. Say it with me:</p><p class="cr">"A channel is how they find you and get it."</p><p class="t">Some channels you own, like your own table at a school event or your own page online. Some channels belong to someone else, like selling on consignment through an existing store. Both are real channels. The moccasin maker from Week 1 used a channel that belonged to someone else, Faught's. Rock'N Outlaw built their own channel, their own building.</p><p class="t">Neither one is better. The right channel depends on your audience. Where do the people you named yesterday already go?</p></div><div class="script"><p class="a"><span class="k">Round 1 — With Support:</span> Word wall visible (value proposition, target audience, functional, emotional, societal, channel). Call a term, students respond chorally, checking the wall as needed.</p><p class="a"><span class="k">Round 2 — Without Support:</span> Cover the word wall. Timed — 60 seconds. Call terms rapid-fire, individual students respond, log speed and accuracy.</p></div><table class="fluency-table"><tr><th>Round</th><th>Support</th><th>Timing</th><th>Response Mode</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>Word wall visible</td><td>Untimed</td><td>Choral</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>None</td><td>60 sec, timed</td><td>Individual, cold-call</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>None</td><td>Untimed</td><td>Peer partner quiz</td></tr></table><h2 class="section">29:00–40:00 · Practice — Two Tracks Running Concurrently</h2><h3 class="subsection">Main Class — Round 3</h3><div class="script"><p class="t">Partner up, quiz each other on all six terms. If your partner is stuck, ask a question that helps them find the answer.</p><p class="a">Circulate and log Not Yet/Developing/Fluent per student based on Rounds 2 and 3 combined.</p></div><div class="sped-block"><h3 class="subsection">SPED Pull-Aside — "Where Do People Already Go?"</h3><p style="margin-top:0;"><strong>Format:</strong> One small group, teacher-led, 2nd-grade reading level.</p><div class="sped-script"><p class="t">If you want to sell something to elders, where would you go, where they already are?</p><p class="a">Picture cards of local gathering places (senior center, school, powwow grounds, a store) matched to a person (elder, student, community member, shopper). Students match the person to the place they already go.</p><p class="s">[names a place]</p><p class="t">That place is a channel. You do not have to build something new. You can go where people already are.</p></div></div><h2 class="section">40:00–45:00 · Exit Check</h2><div class="script"><p class="t">Fast round, no writing: I say a term, you say the definition.</p><p class="a">Not Yet / Developing / Fluent per student.</p></div><table class="assess"><tr><th>Student</th><th>Not Yet</th><th>Developing</th><th>Fluent</th></tr><tr><td colspan="4" style="color:#888;">(roster rows — printed version)</td></tr></table><div class="identity"><h3>You Already Know Where Your People Are</h3><p><span class="cap">Important names are capitalized</span> — and you are an important person. Finding a channel is not about inventing a brand-new place. It is about noticing where the people you already named actually spend their time. Tomorrow we look at keeping them, not just reaching them once.</p></div></div></body></html>
