</style></head><body><header><div class="eyebrow">Entrepreneurship · Browning High School</div><h1>Keeping Them, Not Just Reaching Them</h1><div class="sub">Week 5 · Day 4 — Customer Relationships</div></header><div class="timestrip"><div class="ts-login">0:00–8:00<br>Login + Typing</div><div class="ts-review">8:00–11:00<br>Choral Recall</div><div class="ts-di">11:00–29:00<br>DI Instruction</div><div class="ts-practice">29:00–40:00<br>Practice / SPED Pull-Aside</div><div class="ts-exit">40:00–45:00<br>Exit + Logoff</div></div><div class="wrap"><div class="note goal"><span class="label">Unit Goal Connection</span>Students learn that reaching a customer once and keeping them are two different jobs, closing out the three Week 5 blocks (Value Proposition, Channels, Customer Relationships) before Friday's synthesis. Financial literacy touch: V. Managing Credit; VI. Managing Risk. A repeat customer is a more reliable, lower-risk source of income than a one-time sale.</div><h2 class="section">0:00–8:00 · Login &amp; Typing Practice</h2><p>Standard routine.</p><h2 class="section">8:00–11:00 · Choral Recall</h2><div class="script"><p class="t">Yesterday. A channel is...</p><p class="cr">"How they find you and get it."</p><p class="t">Today, a new question. What happens after someone buys from you once?</p></div><h2 class="section">11:00–29:00 · DI Instruction — I Do / We Do / You Do</h2><h3 class="subsection">I Do — Three Kinds of Customer Relationship</h3><div class="script"><p class="t">A <span class="k">customer relationship</span> is how you treat someone after they find you, not just how you found them. There are a few common kinds. Say them with me:</p><p class="cr">"Self-service. Personal. Dedicated personal."</p><p class="t"><span class="k">Self-service</span> means the customer helps themselves, like a vending machine or an online order with no conversation. <span class="k">Personal</span> means you talk with them, like a shop where the owner knows your name. <span class="k">Dedicated personal</span> means one person is assigned to take care of that customer specifically, like a personal trainer or a loan officer.</p><p class="t">None of these are wrong. The question is which one fits your idea and your audience.</p><p class="t">Here is why this matters for money. A customer who comes back is worth more over time than one who buys once and never returns. Keeping a relationship going is often cheaper than finding a brand new customer every single time.</p></div><h3 class="subsection">We Do — Sorting Examples</h3><div class="script"><p class="t">I will name a business interaction. Tell me which kind of relationship it is.</p><p class="a">Examples: an online order with no messages back and forth (self-service). A shop owner who remembers your order every time (personal). A financial advisor who checks in with you regularly (dedicated personal). A vending machine (self-service).</p><p class="t">Which kind do you think the moccasin maker at Faught's has with a repeat customer?</p><p class="s">[Likely personal, since it is a small, known maker]</p></div><div class="note channel"><span class="label">Learning Channel</span>Hear/Say into Think/Say. Sorting known examples first makes it easier to reason about their own idea next.</div><h3 class="subsection">You Do — Guided Practice</h3><div class="script"><p class="t">Decide which kind of relationship fits your idea best, and write one sentence on how you would actually keep a customer coming back.</p></div><h2 class="section">29:00–40:00 · Practice — Two Tracks Running Concurrently</h2><h3 class="subsection">Main Class — Independent Practice</h3><p>Students finish their relationship type and keep-them-coming-back sentence. Early finishers sketch what a second purchase from the same customer might look like.</p><div class="sped-block"><h3 class="subsection">SPED Pull-Aside — "Would They Come Back?"</h3><p style="margin-top:0;"><strong>Format:</strong> One small group, teacher-led, 2nd-grade reading level.</p><div class="sped-script"><p class="t">If you help someone once and they are happy, would they want your help again?</p><p class="s">[Probably yes]</p><p class="t">What could you do to make sure they come back next time?</p><p class="a">Simple examples: remembering their name, doing a good job, being kind. Use picture cards of a smiling repeat customer versus a one-time customer to reinforce the idea that being remembered matters.</p><p class="s">[names something simple, like being friendly or doing good work]</p></div></div><h2 class="section">40:00–45:00 · Exit Check</h2><div class="script"><p class="t">One sentence: which kind of relationship fits your idea, and how will you keep them coming back?</p><p class="a">Not Yet / Developing / Fluent per student.</p></div><table class="assess"><tr><th>Student</th><th>Not Yet</th><th>Developing</th><th>Fluent</th></tr><tr><td colspan="4" style="color:#888;">(roster rows — printed version)</td></tr></table><div class="identity"><h3>Being Remembered Is a Skill You Already Have</h3><p><span class="cap">Important names are capitalized</span> — and you are an important person. Keeping a relationship going, remembering someone, treating them well, showing up again, is something you already know how to do with people in your own life. Tomorrow we put value proposition, channel, and relationship together in one plan.</p></div></div></body></html>
