</style></head><body><header><div class="eyebrow">Entrepreneurship · Browning High School</div><h1>Value, Channel, Relationship</h1><div class="sub">Week 5 · Day 5 — Portfolio Piece, Preview of Week 6</div></header><div class="timestrip"><div class="ts-login">0:00–8:00<br>Login + Typing</div><div class="ts-review">8:00–11:00<br>Choral Recall</div><div class="ts-di">11:00–29:00<br>DI Instruction</div><div class="ts-practice">29:00–40:00<br>Practice / SPED Pull-Aside</div><div class="ts-exit">40:00–45:00<br>Exit + Logoff</div></div><div class="wrap"><div class="note goal"><span class="label">Unit Goal Connection</span>Finalizes the Week 5 end-of-week artifact: a one-paragraph marketing statement combining the value proposition, target audience, channel, and relationship type into one coherent plan. Feeds Week 6's 4Ps worksheet directly.</div><h2 class="section">0:00–8:00 · Login &amp; Typing Practice</h2><p>Standard routine.</p><h2 class="section">8:00–11:00 · Choral Recall</h2><div class="script"><p class="t">This week. A value proposition is...</p><p class="cr">"A statement of why someone should choose you."</p><p class="t">A channel is...</p><p class="cr">"How they find you and get it."</p><p class="t">And a customer relationship is...</p><p class="cr">"How you treat them after they find you."</p><p class="t">Today, all three become one paragraph.</p></div><h2 class="section">11:00–29:00 · DI Instruction — Finalizing the Marketing Statement</h2><h3 class="subsection">I Do — Modeling the Combined Paragraph</h3><div class="script"><p class="t">Watch me combine four pieces into one paragraph: my value proposition, my target audience, my channel, and my relationship type.</p><p class="a">Model: "My idea helps elders in this community who no longer drive and depend on family who work during the day. I reach them through the senior center, a place they already go. Once someone signs up, I keep a personal relationship, checking in by name so they know who is coming. This saves them the stress of waiting on a ride that might not come."</p><p class="t">Notice every sentence uses something from this week. Nothing is made up on the spot.</p></div><h3 class="subsection">We Do — Peer Feedback Pass</h3><div class="script"><p class="t">Trade workbooks. Read your partner's paragraph. Ask them: which sentence is the target audience, which is the channel, which is the relationship? If they cannot point to one, that part is missing.</p><p class="a">Same peer error-correction structure as previous Friday sessions. The partner asks, does not answer for them.</p></div><div class="note comprehension"><span class="label">Comprehension Tip</span>The most common gap is a paragraph that states the value proposition well but never names an actual place (channel) or how the relationship works. Check for all three pieces, not just polished writing.</div><h3 class="subsection">You Do — Final Write</h3><div class="script"><p class="t">Revise your paragraph using your partner's feedback. This is the version that goes in your portfolio and feeds Week 6.</p></div><h2 class="section">29:00–40:00 · Practice — Two Tracks Running Concurrently</h2><h3 class="subsection">Main Class — Optional Share</h3><p>Ask for 3-4 volunteers to read their paragraph aloud. Not mandatory.</p><div class="sped-block"><h3 class="subsection">SPED Pull-Aside — Finishing the Week</h3><p style="margin-top:0;"><strong>Format:</strong> One small group, teacher-led, 2nd-grade reading level.</p><div class="sped-script"><p class="t">This week you learned who your idea helps, where to find them, and how to keep them coming back. Let's say it together. I help ____. I find them at ____. I keep them coming back by ____.</p><p class="a">Help each student assemble their own sentence orally, then support them writing or dictating it.</p></div></div><h2 class="section">40:00–45:00 · Exit Check</h2><div class="script"><p class="t">Before you log off, next week we take this paragraph and turn it into an actual plan, what you sell, where, for how much, and how you'll promote it. Bring this paragraph back Monday.</p><p class="a">Not Yet / Developing / Fluent, final Week 5 mark, based on whether all three pieces appear in the paragraph.</p></div><table class="assess"><tr><th>Student</th><th>Not Yet</th><th>Developing</th><th>Fluent</th></tr><tr><td colspan="4" style="color:#888;">(roster rows — printed version)</td></tr></table><div class="identity"><h3>This Is a Real Plan, Not Just an Idea Anymore</h3><p><span class="cap">Important names are capitalized</span> — and you are an important person. What you wrote today is not a guess about who might want your idea. It is a plan built from real testing and real thinking about your own community. Next week, it becomes a full marketing plan.</p></div></div></body></html>
